 ### [Projection](7205-projection.md)
-Projection is a direct volume rendering technique that visualizes the total accumulated intensity of a 3D volume along a specific axis by summing voxel values. It projects volume elements onto a 2D screen plane, effectively creating a 2D representation of the 3D data. Unlike [Maximum Intensity Projection](902-maximum-intensity-projection.md), which highlights the brightest structures, projection preserves the total mass or intensity, making it suitable for visualizing diffuse or low-contrast structures where cumulative signal is informative. It is distinct from [Projected Slabs Approximation](1449-projected-slabs-approximation.md) by summing individual samples rather than pre-integrating slabs, and from [Forward Projection (aTx)](1492-forward-projection-atx.md) by lacking attenuation modeling.
+Projection is a direct volume rendering technique that visualizes the total accumulated intensity of a 3D volume along a specific axis by summing voxel values. It solves the problem of compressing volumetric data into a 2D representation where the brightness of each pixel corresponds to the integral of the scalar field along the viewing ray. Unlike [Maximum Intensity Projection](902-maximum-intensity-projection.md), which highlights the highest density structures, projection preserves the global distribution of intensity but significantly reduces image contrast due to the cumulative effect of background activity.
 
 ### [Maximum Intensity Projection (MIP)](10702-maximum-intensity-projection-mip.md)
-Maximum Intensity Projection (MIP) is a volume rendering technique that projects the maximum scalar value encountered along each viewing ray onto the 2D image plane. It is primarily used to highlight high-intensity structures, such as blood vessels in angiography or bone in CT scans, by suppressing lower-intensity background noise. Unlike standard front-to-back compositing which blends all samples, MIP discards all intermediate values in favor of the single maximum, which causes it to lose depth differentiation and the ability to distinguish whether the maximum lies in the front or back of the volume.
+Maximum Intensity Projection (MIP) projects the maximum scalar value encountered along each viewing ray onto the 2D image plane. Unlike standard alpha blending, which composites translucent surfaces and can obscure high-intensity features behind lower-intensity ones, MIP highlights the brightest structures in the dataset, making it particularly effective for visualizing high-contrast features such as blood vessels in angiography or bone structures in CT scans. It serves as a fast overview mode that reveals intensity distributions that might be lost in alpha blending.
 
 ### [Attenuated Maximum-Intensity Projection](16662-attenuated-maximum-intensity-projection.md)
-Attenuated Maximum-Intensity Projection (Attenuated MIP) projects the maximum intensity along rays while suppressing contributions from deep or weak samples using an exponential falloff based on accumulated intensity. Unlike standard [Maximum Intensity Projection](10702-maximum-intensity-projection-mip.md) which simply selects the highest value regardless of depth, Attenuated MIP dims background haze and weaker structures, ensuring that only strong, distinct peaks survive the projection. It is chosen when the goal is to highlight high-intensity features without the visual clutter of overlapping lower-intensity volumes that often obscures detail in standard MIP.
+Attenuated Maximum-Intensity Projection (AMIP) is a CPU-emulated volume rendering technique that enhances the visibility of high-intensity structures by suppressing background noise and weaker signals that typically obscure details in standard Maximum-Intensity Projection (MIP). Unlike standard MIP, which simply selects the highest intensity value along a ray regardless of depth, AMIP weights samples by an exponential falloff based on the accumulated intensity encountered so far, ensuring that only strong, isolated peaks survive while dimmer, deeper contributions are attenuated. This method is particularly useful for visualizing dense volumetric data where background haze would otherwise bleed over and obscure critical features.
 
 ### [MPI Defocus Rendering](17207-mpi-defocus-rendering.md)
-MPI Defocus Rendering synthesizes images with realistic depth-of-field effects and lateral camera translation by compositing a precomputed Multi-Plane Image (MPI) representation. It differs from standard ray casting by operating on discrete disparity planes rather than continuous volumetric samples, allowing for efficient simulation of defocus blur and parallax through 2D image-space convolutions and translations. This method is specifically designed for view synthesis from trained neural radiance fields (NeRFs), particularly those trained on raw sensor data, where it avoids the computational cost of per-pixel ray marching for every novel view.
+MPI Defocus Rendering synthesizes images with realistic depth-of-field blur and lateral camera translation by compositing a precomputed Multi-Plane Image (MPI) representation. It differs from standard ray casting by operating on discrete disparity planes rather than continuous volumetric samples, allowing for efficient simulation of defocus blur via 2D image-space convolutions. This method is specifically designed for view synthesis from trained neural radiance fields (NeRFs), particularly in low-light conditions where raw sensor data is utilized.
 
-### Perspective Approximation via Orthogonal Slices
-An approximation for perspective volume rendering that uses view-orthogonal slices instead of concentric spherical shells, which are expensive to generate [S2]. The only difference from exact ray casting is that the distances of sample positions along a ray depend on the ray's orientation [S2]. Exact perspective ray casting produces associated sample positions forming concentric spherical shells, not planes. Generating these shells is expensive [S2].
+#### Perspective Approximation via Orthogonal Slices
+An approximation for perspective volume rendering that uses view-orthogonal slices instead of concentric spherical shells, which are expensive to generate [S2]. The only difference from exact ray casting is that the distances of sample positions along a ray depend on the ray's orientation [S2]. Exact perspective ray casting produces associated sample positions forming concentric spherical shells, not planes, and generating these shells is expensive [S2].
 
