 ## Background
-The concept of Maximum Intensity Projection (MIP) emerged in the early 1990s as a standard tool for viewing multi-detector CT (MDCT) and magnetic resonance angiography (MRA) data, allowing radiologists to view high-contrast structures like blood vessels or bones without the clutter of surrounding soft tissue [S1, unofficial][S2][S3, unofficial][S4]. The technique is order-independent, meaning the result does not depend on the order in which samples are processed, which facilitates parallelization and hardware acceleration [S2, S4].
-
-Projected Slabs Approximation was developed to address the limitations of early hardware that only supported orthographic projection, allowing perspective views to be rendered efficiently by decomposing the volume into parallel slabs [S1, unofficial]. Forward Projection (aTx) mimics the physical process of X-ray attenuation, summing density values along rays to create projection images similar to conventional radiography [S1, unofficial]. Minimum Intensity Projection (MinIP) serves as the inverse to MIP, highlighting low-density structures such as airways or signal voids [S1, unofficial][S2].
-
-Attenuated MIP was introduced to mitigate the "clutter" problem in standard MIP, where overlapping low-intensity structures can obscure high-intensity features [S1, unofficial]. MPI Defocus Rendering is a more recent technique leveraging Multi-Plane Images for efficient depth-of-field simulation in neural rendering contexts [S1, unofficial].
+MIP was introduced as a standard technique in medical imaging visualization to overcome the limitations of alpha blending in highlighting high-contrast structures. The term "MIP_Mode" is used in interactive visualization libraries to denote this specific rendering mode [S1, unofficial]. The technique is widely supported in medical imaging workstations and is a fundamental tool for angiography and bone visualization.
 
